WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.750
Throughout his career, M. Night Shyamalan
has managed to carve out a distinct filmography

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.840
filled with stylized genre efforts - featuring
compelling characters, extraordinary mysteries,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.540
and some of the greatest twists in the history
of cinema.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.560
Here are all of M. Night Shyamalan's movies,
ranked from worst to best.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.340
M. Night Shyamalan built his reputation on
crafting thoughtful blockbuster films with

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.860
non-blockbuster budgets.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.600
Of his first seven films, none cost more than
$75 million to make.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.570
So it was a little surprising that on the
heels of two major flops, Shyamalan managed

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.640
to procure a whopping $150 million for his
adaptation of the fantastical adventure The

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.700
Last Airbender.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.600
Maybe he just couldn't the resist the temptation
to go bigger than he'd ever gone before, but

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.540
those who bought a ticket to see The Last
Airbender were left wondering exactly where

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:47.320
that money went.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.079
A wave of abysmal reviews greeted the movie’s
release.

00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:55.540
Considering that Airbender is based on a wildly
inventive Nickelodeon series, you’ve got

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:00.149
to marvel at the sheer dullness of this adaptation,
which is driven by soulless performances,

00:01:00.149 --> 00:01:04.360
stale visuals, uninspired set pieces, and
unbelievably bad special effects.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:17.920
Needless to say, initial plans for a Shyamalan-directed
Airbender trilogy were promptly halted, though

00:01:17.929 --> 00:01:22.540
the film still fared reasonably well at the
box office: in fact, it managed to rake in

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.900
north of $300 million worldwide, which may
be the biggest twist Shyamalan has ever pulled.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.920
Prior to beginning production on his 2006
fairytale Lady In The Water, Shyamalan was

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.549
on one hell of a winning streak, with two
instant classics to his credit and a couple

00:01:37.549 --> 00:01:39.440
of solid box office hits to boot.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:44.420
So Lady In The Water, which boasted a cast
fronted by Paul Giamatti, Bryce Dallas Howard

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:48.759
and Jeffrey Wright, made its premiere aiming
for a higher bar than even Shyamalan might've

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:49.320
understood.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.159
Needless to say, critics were less than kind
to Shyamalan's bonkers yarn about a blue-collar

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.760
guy trying to help a stranded water faerie
return to her homeworld.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.440
Sadly, it’s hard to disagree with those
reviews.

00:02:00.669 --> 00:02:05.260
Though Lady In The Water features stunning
visual elements, the story is a wildly unfocused

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:09.960
confection of kooky characters, half-baked
fantasy elements, and contrived plot twists.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.900
Lady In The Water sunk with audiences too,
becoming Shyamalan's first legit box office

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.260
bomb - and one of his most disappointing efforts
to date.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.880
Shyamalan began work on his debut film Praying
With Anger while still a student at NYU.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.760
He wrote, produced, funded, directed, and
starred in the project himself.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.550
It revolves around an Americanized young man
of East Indian descent returning home to rediscover

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.940
his roots - and Shyamalan, who was born in
India and raised in an affluent suburb of

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.670
Philadelphia, based Praying With Anger on
his own return trip to India in his teens.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:44.180
Needless to say, it was an intensely personal
story.

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.940
Praying With Anger has a warmth and sense
of humor all but absent from much of his later

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:49.440
work.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:52.600
As with most debuts, however, it isn't without
its problems.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.220
The movie suffers from tone and pacing issues,
and Shyamalan - never a particularly strong

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:01.560
performer - proves he likely took on one job
too many by casting himself in the lead.

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:07.180
Whether he made that decision based on necessity
or on hubris, Shyamalan's flat, detached performance

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.760
is often painful to watch.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.840
“I just decided I wanted to see as much
of India as I can.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.460
There are a lot of beautiful things in this
culture.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.800
Really beautiful.”

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:22.100
Luckily, there's enough going on beneath the
surface of Praying With Anger to keep it engaging.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.120
At the very least, it's a compelling look
at a first-time director figuring out just

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:27.040
how movies work.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.580
Even after the costly debacle that was 2010's
The Last Airbender, Shyamalan wasn't quite

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.569
done swinging for the big-budget fences - and
Hollywood studios weren't quite done betting

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:37.780
big on the director, either.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:44.040
Columbia Pictures ponied up $130 million for
Shyamalan's sci-fi adventure After Earth,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:47.540
and although the reviews weren't quite as
abysmal as they had been for his most recent

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.740
efforts, they weren't exactly good.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.820
That's not totally surprising.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:56.620
After Earth was originally pitched to Shyamalan
by Will Smith as a star vehicle for himself

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:00.530
and his son Jaden, and follows the pair as
they struggle for survival after being shipwrecked

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:01.560
on an alien planet.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:06.840
It's a promising setup for the Shyamalan treatment,
but the surprising lack of chemistry between

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:11.780
the real-life father and son leaves an emotional
void in that the movie struggles to overcome.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.500
For his part, Shyamalan once again drops the
ball when it comes to the film's staging and

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:20.180
visual elements, wasting the talents of brilliant
cinematographer Peter Suschitzky along the

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:20.680
way.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.160
Though After Earth bombed hard stateside,
it did well enough overseas to turn a modest

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:25.660
profit.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.460
Still, given his track record with tentpole
fare, we're betting it'll be awhile before

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:31.380
Shyamalan goes this big again.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.420
After giving his fanbase cause for concern
with Lady In The Water, Shyamalan continued

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:41.080
his stumble with The Happening, a sci-fi thriller
that follows a teacher, his wife, and their

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.420
friends as they try to outrun a mysterious
plague.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.729
On paper, the concept has the makings of another
intriguing Twilight Zone-ish tale for Shyamalan.

00:04:48.729 --> 00:04:53.400
But the film features career-worst performances
from Mark Wahlberg and Zooey Deschanel, a

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:58.430
third act that can only be described as mind-bogglingly
stupid, and the sort of hackneyed, tin-eared

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.920
writing that leaves one wondering how The
Happening got off the ground at all.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:03.340
“My name is Elliot Moore.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:10.160
I’m just going to talk in a very positive
manner, giving off good vibes.”

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.479
And there's a reason why this movie feels
unpolished: Shyamalan was working on the script

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.540
while the 2007 writers’ strike loomed over
Hollywood.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:22.120
Fearing a lengthy content drought, studios
began fast-tracking productions to fill a

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:24.870
potential void - and The Happening was one
of them.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.199
It was released to some of the worst reviews
of Shyamalan's career, but the one thing it

00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:34.219
has got going for it is a campy, irreverent,
B-movie charm that never quite lets you take

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.440
the onscreen absurdity too seriously.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.300
That B-movie feel may not be intentional,
but it's the only reason The Happening is

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.680
at all watchable, and ultimately keeps it
from claiming the title of "Worst Shyamalan

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:45.180
Movie."

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.300
"Why ya eyein’ my lemon drink?"

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:54.139
In 1998, Shyamalan returned to the director's
chair for his family-friendly dramedy Wide

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:54.640
Awake.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.759
The plot follows a fifth grader who sets out
on a search for God after the death of his

00:05:58.760 --> 00:05:59.260
grandfather.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:04.419
Though Wide Awake never quite hits home, straying
too often into the realms of shameless schmaltz,

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:08.960
it more than proved the director's skill at
tackling complex dramatic content in a clever

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.980
and insightful way - as well as his ability
to coax convincing performances from his cast.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:18.599
Shyamalan's ability to work with younger actors
in particular would prove important for his

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:19.180
next movie.

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:24.779
It's hard to believe how many years have passed
since Shyamalan turned the superhero genre

00:06:24.779 --> 00:06:29.969
on its head with his gloriously grounded origin
story Unbreakable - and it's still surprising

00:06:29.969 --> 00:06:34.990
how sneakily Night shocked the world by connecting
his unhinged thriller Split to the same cinematic

00:06:34.990 --> 00:06:35.480
universe.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.520
Yet here we are, bearing witness to the rarest
of big-screen events - a comic book-centric

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.840
crossover based on wholly original heroes
and villains.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:47.759
Folks going into Glass expecting a heroes
versus villains throwdown on the level of

00:06:47.759 --> 00:06:52.490
the latest Marvel confection will be seriously
underwhelmed by the meticulously paced action

00:06:52.490 --> 00:06:53.560
unfolding before them.

00:06:54.029 --> 00:06:58.600
But those expecting a superpowered psychological
thriller akin to Unbreakable and Split will

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:03.150
find themselves right at home amongst Glass'
morally twisted tale of good and evil.

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:07.159
The movie’s ambition ultimately trumps its
quality, mostly thanks to some spotty writing

00:07:07.159 --> 00:07:12.169
by Shyamalan himself, but it also takes more
risks, has more fun, and features higher stakes

00:07:12.169 --> 00:07:14.580
than any film spawned by the MCU.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:20.620
Signs might be overly-long, but it's as close
to a sci-fi masterpiece as anything Shyamalan

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:21.340
has ever delivered.

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:25.880
It also finds the director skillfully blending
his penchant for spellbinding mysteries and

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.280
paranoia-drenched narratives with his go-to
themes of families in strife and crises of

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:34.320
faith - all to tell an alien invasion story
with multiple twists.

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:39.040
This tale of a fallen preacher struggling
with his faith as a largely unseen alien menace

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:43.089
threatens to rip his family apart is taut,
tense, heartfelt, and harrowing.

00:07:43.089 --> 00:07:47.810
It's also bolstered by one of Mel Gibson's
finest performances, not to mention a scene-stealing

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:49.400
turn from Joaquin Phoenix.

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:03.699
For much of its runtime, Signs is a near flawless
blend of horror-tinged atmospherics, classic

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:08.259
alien invasion tropes, and tragic religious
parable that's unafraid to wear its heart

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:09.960
or its influences on its sleeve.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:15.180
If only the final 10 minutes of the movie
didn't exist, it might have had a legit claim

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.180
to the top spot on this list.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:21.219
Sadly, that ending does exist, and if you've
seen it you probably know why Signs doesn't

00:08:21.219 --> 00:08:22.909
even earn a place in the top five.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:28.060
The Village might be the most underrated film
in Shyamalan's big bag of movies.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:30.839
Yes, its central conceit is shamelessly manipulative.

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.940
Yes, the film's big twists are underwhelming
and obvious.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:37.539
But while those issues are impossible to ignore,
they're not really the point.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:42.500
They exist more as fan-serviced genre fodder
and structural plot devices than focal points

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:43.620
of the actual story.

00:08:43.940 --> 00:08:46.780
That story happens to be one of Shyamalan’s
best.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.080
It’s a mournful and gothic chamber piece
bent on exploring the weighty correlation

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:55.400
between grief and fear, presented in the guise
of a straight-up horror tale.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:59.840
The plot finds an isolated village on edge
when the mystical creatures who dwell in the

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:03.420
surrounding woods appear to break the terms
of a long-standing treaty.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:08.960
Shyamalan wisely focuses on interpersonal
drama, allowing Roger Deakins' stark, expressive

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:13.510
photography and James Newton Howard's dolefully
romantic score to bestow The Village with

00:09:13.510 --> 00:09:15.800
an emotive yet disquieting unease.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.440
In the process, he tells one of his most patently
human stories - one that's well worth revisiting.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:27.620
After reeling off his fourth straight big-budget
misfire in After Earth, even Shyamalan seemed

00:09:27.620 --> 00:09:28.880
to think a break was in order.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:33.660
Fortunately for film lovers everywhere, he
used his brief downtime to recharge his imagination

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:35.750
and refine his cinematic approach.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:40.650
When he returned with 2015's pseudo found-footage
thriller The Visit, he proved he was ready

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.220
to reclaim the title of genre maestro.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:48.620
The Visit is every bit the lean, focused,
genre-jumping mood-fest we'd come to expect

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:49.800
from Shyamalan's early work.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:54.340
Only this story, which is centered on teenage
siblings visiting their grandparents and finding

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:57.880
them entrenched in some seriously strange
behavior, feels a little different.

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:02.700
It's dangerous and even a little depraved
in ways we hadn't really seen from Shyamalan

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:03.200
before.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:08.440
The Visit feels like Shyamalan finally giving
over to the paranoid, claustrophobic menace

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:12.270
he'd only touched upon in his prior films,
while the movie’s handheld vantage points

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.640
only heightened the broiling anxiety underneath.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:20.010
That unexpected mix of anxiousness and dark
danger heralded the second coming of a filmmaker

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:24.561
who'd found a way to tweak his signature style
to feel as energetic and exhilarating as it

00:10:24.561 --> 00:10:25.500
did in the beginning.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:30.600
The Visit didn't quite put Shyamalan back
on top, but it proved he was finally back

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:31.180
in the game.

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:36.400
Armed with an edgy new approach and backing
from a risk-taking studio in Blumhouse, Shyamalan

00:10:36.400 --> 00:10:38.920
was about to get down and dirty with a new
genre medley.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.560
It would turn out to be one of the wildest,
creepiest rides of his career.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:50.700
“The impure are the untouched, the unburned,
the unslain!”

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:55.760
Packed with more twists than any previous
Shyamalan film and anchored by an eerily transfixing

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:59.980
performance from James McAvoy, Split follows
the nightmarish tale of a man with multiple

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:01.160
personality disorder.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:05.820
As he struggles to find balance in his fractured
mind, a dangerous new personality threatens

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:08.890
to take control and force him to do unspeakable
things.

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:13.490
It's a brilliant blend of psychological thriller
and straight up horror, and it earned Shyamalan

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:17.780
earned of the best reviews and box office
returns of his career - all while unexpectedly

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:22.040
leading into a belated sequel of one of his
most widely acclaimed films.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:27.080
Unbreakable arrived in theaters in the fall
of 2000 behind a cryptic trailer and under

00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:28.420
a thick shroud of secrecy.

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:32.540
Over the years, it has proceeded to blow the
minds of pretty much everyone who's ever seen

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:33.040
it.

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:36.940
There's really not much to say about Unbreakable
that hasn't already been said, except that

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:41.130
it's one of those rare films that remains
consistently re-watchable even after you've

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.600
uncovered all of its secrets.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.660
That the film contains what may well stand
as career-best work from Bruce Willis and

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:51.660
Samuel L. Jackson is a big reason for the
film's success, but Unbreakable's stylishly

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:56.440
muted, hyper-realistic approach to the superhero
origin story is a larger part of why it remains

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:59.100
one of the brighter highlights in Shyamalan's
filmography.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.080
Not exactly a twist ending to this list, but
what else could land the top spot?

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:08.920
No matter how many superb movies Shyamalan
might make, it will always comes back to his

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.340
breakout supernatural hit: The Sixth Sense.

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:15.380
Twist ending aside, perhaps the most surprising
thing about The Sixth Sense is that it was

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:19.160
released only about a year after his so-so
family dramedy Wide Awake.

00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:23.110
It seems that all Shyamalan needed to find
his groove was a couple of ghosts and one

00:12:23.110 --> 00:12:24.120
terrified 10-year-old.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:29.180
The Sixth Sense's tale of a kind-hearted boy
who sees dead people found the director working

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:30.720
with a new level of confidence.

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:35.440
It also found him coaxing a career re-defining
performance out of Bruce Willis and one of

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:39.630
the greatest child performances in history
from a young Haley Joel Osment.

00:12:39.630 --> 00:12:43.840
Yes, that twist is the stuff of legend, but
the story still works even when you already

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:44.500
know what's coming.

00:12:44.780 --> 00:12:48.811
Perhaps what sets Shyamalan's supernatural
puzzle box apart from the pack is that, no

00:12:48.811 --> 00:12:52.900
matter how many times you watch it, The Sixth
Sense always seems to offer something new

00:12:52.900 --> 00:12:53.520
to discover.

